WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.236
Secure firmware in Rust is a layered
lifecycle practice.

2
00:00:03.316 --> 00:00:06.964
Start with assets, interfaces, boundaries,
physical access assumptions,

3
00:00:07.044 --> 00:00:10.407
protected functions, field maintenance,
and end-of-life behavior.

4
00:00:10.487 --> 00:00:14.419
These assumptions should become
requirements that the team can implement, test,

5
00:00:14.499 --> 00:00:15.183
and maintain.

6
00:00:15.263 --> 00:00:17.543
Prefer safe Rust abstractions where
possible,

7
00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:21.499
and keep hardware-facing unsafe code
small, justified, documented, reviewed,

8
00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:22.776
and wrapped in safe APIs.

9
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:26.105
Register behavior, peripheral ownership,
initialization order,

10
00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:30.118
interrupt interactions, and platform
dependencies should be visible enough for

11
00:00:30.198 --> 00:00:31.566
another engineer to review.

12
00:00:31.646 --> 00:00:35.750
Bound memory, queues, parsing, sizes,
timeouts, update states, and recovery paths.

13
00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.938
Safe Rust reduces memory-corruption risk,

14
00:00:38.018 --> 00:00:41.951
but secure firmware also needs resource
limits, predictable degraded behavior,

15
00:00:42.031 --> 00:00:44.482
and clear panic or recovery policy under
pressure.

16
00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:46.386
Treat parsing as a security boundary.

17
00:00:46.466 --> 00:00:48.803
Validate length, framing, fields, mode,
state,

18
00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:51.106
and relationships before sensitive
actions.

19
00:00:51.186 --> 00:00:55.062
Convert valid input into domain types so
later code operates on reviewed meaning

20
00:00:55.142 --> 00:00:57.023
rather than raw bytes and assumptions.

21
00:00:57.103 --> 00:00:58.642
Finally, harden the lifecycle.

22
00:00:58.722 --> 00:01:02.312
Protect firmware integrity, update
behavior, rollback policy, secrets,

23
00:01:02.392 --> 00:01:06.211
device identity, debug interfaces,
diagnostics, dependencies, toolchains,

24
00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:09.540
builds, artifacts, releases, and
maintenance responsibilities.

25
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.356
Test boundary cases, failure paths,
recovery behavior,

26
00:01:12.436 --> 00:01:14.260
and lifecycle assumptions over time.
